@node Compatibility
@chapter Compatibility with the original xwpe

@cindex compatibility
@cindex differences from xwpe 1.4

This chapter lists what xwpe now does differently from the xwpe of the
1990s, where a user of the original would notice it.  Each entry says
what the original did, what xwpe does now, and why.

@section While the debugger answers at a stop

@table @asis
@item Ctrl-C while the debugger answers
@cindex Ctrl-C, while gdb answers
The original asked, in a box, whether to quit the debugger when
@kbd{Ctrl-C} was typed while it read the debugger's answer.  Under gdb,
pdb, jdb, a68g, sdb, dbx and xdb, @kbd{Ctrl-C} now stops waiting for the
answer, as @key{Esc}
does, and the status row says which command the debugger did not answer;
Debug > Quit ends the session.  The box is gone: the editor keeps the
keyboard while the debugger answers, at a stop, while the debugger starts
or when a run or a step goes out, so there is no moment where only a box
could get you out.

@item Keys typed while the debugger answers
@cindex keys, while gdb answers
The original passed every key typed while it read the debugger's answer
on to the debugger.  Under gdb, pdb, jdb, a68g, sdb, dbx and xdb, keys
typed then are the editor's: text
goes into the file, menus open, and a debugger command waits for the
answer or is refused with the waiting line.  A key that edits acts at
once, so it may act before the step typed just before it has stopped.
Keys that reached the debugger behind the editor's back were how an
answer could be lost or a letter typed into the source.
@end table

@section When the editor ends during a debug session

@table @asis
@item The debugger at the editor's end
@cindex debugger, at the editor's end
The original sent the debugger signal 7 when it ended during a session,
which on Linux is SIGBUS: the debugger died with a core file and could
leave its program running.  xwpe now ends it as Debug > Quit does: told
to quit at its prompt, or sent SIGTERM while its program runs, and
killed only if it is still there two seconds later, so the debugger ends
its program and leaves no core file.
@end table

@section Where the program under the debugger writes

@table @asis
@item The program's terminal
@cindex debugger, terminal of the program
The original terminal editor refused a session unless its own input was
a terminal, and sent the program's output there, over the editor's
screen.  xwpe gives the program a
pseudoterminal of its own and reads its output through it, so a session
starts even when the editor has no terminal, as when it is started from a
desktop launcher.  The editor's terminal is used only when no
pseudoterminal could be opened.
@end table

@section Keys

@table @asis
@item F11 and F12 under X
@cindex F11, F12
The original read the key symbols of @key{F11} and @key{F12} as the Sun
keyboard's Stop and Again keys, which they also are, so a PC keyboard's
@key{F11} quit the editor and @key{F12} redid.  They now do nothing, as
they always did in a terminal: one key gives one action on every display,
and quitting on a key no menu names surprised users.

@item Alt-Delete in a terminal
@cindex Alt-Delete, in a terminal
The original read Esc then Delete in a terminal as Shift-Delete and cut
the marked block.  It is now @kbd{Alt-Delete} on every display, which
copies the block (to the system selection on X11 and Wayland, to the
editor's clipboard in a terminal) and leaves it in the file.

@item Alt-Insert and Alt-Delete under X
@cindex Alt-Insert, under X
The original had a paste and a copy of their own for the X selection.
They now run Paste and Copy, so they behave as @kbd{Ctrl-V} and
@kbd{Ctrl-Insert} do on every display, with five differences under X:
@kbd{Alt-Insert} pastes the editor's clipboard when no application owns
the selection (it pasted nothing); after whole lines were copied it pastes
them as whole lines (it joined the last one to the next line); a window
closed to changes, such as the clipboard viewer, refuses it before
anything changes; a block that ends at the start of a line is copied
without that last line break, as @kbd{Ctrl-Insert} copies it; and
@kbd{Alt-Delete} in the clipboard viewer copies nothing, as
@kbd{Ctrl-Insert} there (it gave the viewer's text to the X selection
again).  @kbd{Ctrl-V} and @kbd{Alt-Insert} take the newest copy on X11 and
Wayland: another application's @kbd{Ctrl-C} text wins over the editor's
own clipboard when it came later, and the editor's clipboard wins over
another application's primary selection.  The editor used to paste its own
text for as long as it still owned the primary selection.  The reason
is one clipboard path for every display, so no display has a copy of its
own to get wrong.
@end table

@section What arrives while a box is open

@table @asis
@item Output, stops and answers under a dialog or a menu
@cindex dialog, output while open
The original did nothing in the background: while a build ran, the
debugger started or the program ran, the editor waited, so no menu or
dialog could be open when output or a stop arrived.  xwpe keeps the
keyboard meanwhile, so a box, the menu bar, a prefix such as
@kbd{Ctrl-G} or the User Screen can be open when they arrive; what
arrives then is kept and drawn when it closes (@pxref{Interface tour}).
The same holds for the program a run starts and for what the loop brings
(the build's output, a reply, a language server's news): the original had
no such callbacks, and here none draws while a box or a run has the screen.
A box drawn over is a box you can no longer read.
@end table

@section Undo and breakpoints

@table @asis
@item Enter with auto-indent
@cindex undo, auto-indent
The original needed two @kbd{Ctrl-U} for one @key{Return} with
auto-indent (the indent, then the line break), and its Redo indented the
line a second time.  It is now one step each way, because one key is one
action.

@item Undo of Ctrl-Y
The original marked the restored line as a block and left the cursor at
its start.  Undo now puts the cursor and the block back where they were
before the deletion, so a block the deletion did not touch survives.

@item The block's end on a deleted line
When the line holding the end of the block was deleted, the original moved
the end to the end of the line above.  It now goes to the start of the line
that took the deleted line's place (to the end of the file when the last
line went), as the block's beginning always did, so both ends of a block
follow the same rule.

@item Ctrl-Y on the only line
The original cleared the only line of a file without recording it, so
@kbd{Ctrl-U} could not bring it back.  It is now undoable like any other
deleted line.

@item Breakpoints on deleted or joined lines
@cindex breakpoints, undo
The original removed the breakpoint of a deleted line (@kbd{Ctrl-Y}, or
@key{Backspace} joining it to the line above) for good, and kept a
breakpoint on its line number when lines were added above it by a wrapped
line or by a tool's rewrite of the file.  The breakpoint now comes back
with Undo, and stays on its code when lines move, so the program stops
where the margin shows.  During a debugging session the debugger is told
of the move too, at once at a stop and after its answer while it is
answering, so a key that moves a breakpoint is never refused for it.

@item Block copy, move and delete
@cindex breakpoints, block operations
@cindex undo, block operations
The original left every breakpoint on its line number when a block was
copied, moved or pasted above it, and dropped the breakpoints of a deleted
block for good; a Search > Replace that made its line wrap left the
breakpoint below on the wrapped tail.  Now a breakpoint stays on its code:
lines a copy or a paste adds push it down, a moved line takes its
breakpoint along (a block that starts at column 0 owns its first line, so
that line's breakpoint moves too when the block is dropped at the start of
a line), and Undo and Redo of a block delete bring the breakpoints of its
lines back and take them away again.  A copy carries no breakpoint.  As in
the original, a breakpoint on a line whose text is merged into another
line goes, and Undo does not bring that one back: the last line of a
block that ends inside a line, when the block is deleted or moved, and
the first line of a block that starts at column 0, when it is dropped
inside a line.

@item Undo of a block moved inside its own line
The original's @kbd{Ctrl-U} of a block moved to an earlier place on the same
line put it past the end of the line, and @kbd{Ctrl-R} of one moved to a
later place put it too far.  Both now give back the line as it was.  A
block copied or moved into a line as wide as the window, which then
wraps, was marked as if the line had not wrapped, so its @kbd{Ctrl-U} took
away characters of yours beside it; it is now marked on the rows it is on.

@item A block operation without memory
@cindex out of memory, block operations
The original stopped a block move, copy, paste or delete that ran out of
memory with a low-memory box that offered to save.  Now such an
operation, its @kbd{Ctrl-U} and its @kbd{Ctrl-R}
are made whole or not at all: refused, the text, the block, the
breakpoints and Undo are as they were, and the status row says
@samp{Out of memory: the block move was not made, the text is
unchanged}.  A file that cannot be read for want of memory is not opened,
so it cannot be saved over as an empty window.

@item Where a moved or pasted block wraps
A line that a block move, copy, paste or delete makes, together with the
rows its soft-wrapped line goes on to, is wrapped where the file reader
wraps a long line, at the last blank or dash that fits; the original
wrapped it as its typing did, a word earlier.  The file written is the
same.

@item Change Case
@cindex undo, change case
The original changed the case of a block (@kbd{Ctrl-K D}) without
recording it, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} could not take it back.  It is now one undo
step, with the breakpoints and the block left where they were.

@item Formats, renames and accepted Edits
@cindex breakpoints, format
@cindex undo, format
The original had no tool that rewrote the whole file.  Here a format, a
rename, a code action and an accepted assistant Edit change only the lines
that differ, and a line that only changed its indentation counts as the same
line.  A breakpoint, the block and the colours stay on their code when the
tool adds, removes or re-indents lines, and one @kbd{Ctrl-U} gives back the
whole change.  Too different a rewrite (more than about two thousand lines
added or removed) is taken as one change to the lines in the middle, and
there breakpoints stay on their line numbers.

@item A file read into the middle of a window
@cindex undo, Block Read
@cindex breakpoints, Block Read
The original split the line at the cursor and put the file's lines in by
hand (@kbd{Ctrl-K R}), recording nothing: @kbd{Ctrl-U} took back whatever
you had typed before, not the insert, the window did not read as modified
because of it, and the breakpoints below stayed on their line numbers.  The
insert is now one undo step, the window reads as modified, and the
breakpoints below move down with their lines, as they do for a paste.

@item The Stack and Watches windows
@cindex undo, Stack and Watches
The original recorded the rows it took out of the Stack window, so
@key{Edit}, @key{Undo} there put back a row of an earlier listing.  What
the debugger printed is not yours to undo: those windows are rewritten at
every stop, and Undo in them says there is nothing to undo.

@item A stack that gets shorter
The original left every other row of the old listing in the Stack window
when the stack lost two or more frames.  The window now shows the new
listing and nothing else.
@end table

@section Editing keys

@table @asis
@item Ctrl-T at the end of the text
@cindex Ctrl-T, end of the text
The original deleted the last line break of the file when @kbd{Ctrl-T}
was pressed at the end of the text, and a second @kbd{Ctrl-T} put the
cursor at the start of the last line.  As in Turbo Pascal and Borland C++,
@kbd{Ctrl-T} there now deletes nothing and the cursor stays: there is no
word and no line to its right.

@item Ctrl-Z at the end of a line
@cindex Ctrl-Z, end of a line
The original recorded an undo step of nothing for @kbd{Ctrl-Z} or
@kbd{Ctrl-O Y} at the end of a line, so the next @kbd{Ctrl-U} took back
nothing, and on an empty last row it deleted the row.  It now deletes
nothing and records nothing there, as the Borland editors do, so
@kbd{Ctrl-U} takes back the change before it.

@item A blank typed at the right margin
The original kept a blank typed at the margin at the end of its row and
wrapped what followed.  The blank now starts the next row with what is
typed after it, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} takes back exactly what was typed, in one
place.  The file written is the same.

@item Rows as wide as Max. Columns
@cindex Max. Columns, row width
@cindex word wrap, typing
The original wrapped typed text two columns before Max.@: Columns, could
lose a character typed where a row had to wrap or cut one outside ASCII
in two, showed a line exactly Max.@: Columns long as a full row with an
empty row below it, and could cut a long line inside a character outside
ASCII when it read a file.  A row now holds Max.@: Columns bytes, the line
break after them, and typing wraps at the next byte, as the manual says:
the row is cut after the last blank or dash that leaves room, else before
the margin, never inside a character, and what you type goes in whole,
on the row below when the cut falls at or before it.  A line read from a
file is cut the same way; a blank in the first two columns counts too.
@key{Backspace} that joins a line which exactly fills what the row above
has left takes the whole line and its line break.  Max.@: Columns counts
bytes: a character outside ASCII takes two to four.  A file whose last
line is a multiple of Max.@: Columns long and has no final line break
gains one when saved, as every other file without one already does.  One
part of the editor now decides how wide a row is, by the manual's own
rule.  A replacement (Search > Replace) or a completion longer than
Max.@: Columns goes in whole over as many rows as it needs, and
@kbd{Ctrl-U} takes it out again; the replace that follows goes on behind
it, also when it wrapped.

@item Overwrite over a character outside ASCII
@cindex overwrite, multibyte characters
The original wrote a character typed in overwrite mode over one byte, so
typed over an @samp{é} or a @samp{€} it left the rest of that character
behind as bytes that are no text.  It now replaces the whole character
under the cursor, and @kbd{Ctrl-U} gives that character back whole.

@item The block Undo marks around a line break
@cindex undo, block marks
The original's @kbd{Ctrl-U} of a deletion that took a line break marked
the text it gave back up to a column past the end of its row, and a
@kbd{Ctrl-K C} or @kbd{Ctrl-K V} of that block followed by @kbd{Ctrl-U}
could lose a line.  The block now ends at the start of the next row, the place
just after the line break, and after any key no mark, and no place a
Shift key selects from, is past the end of its row.  So a Shift selection
made after typing at the end of a line in overwrite mode no longer lets
@kbd{Ctrl-O H}, @kbd{U} or @kbd{M} put a byte behind the line break,
where Save lost it.

@item The tenth marker
@cindex markers, ten
The original kept markers 0 to 8 and let marker 9 share its place with the
search highlight, so it moved whenever another marker was set.  There are
now ten markers, 0 to 9, each with a place of its own, as the Borland
editors have ten bookmarks.

@item Commands in a read-only window
@cindex read-only window, commands
The original let the block and line commands change a file opened
read-only and the clipboard viewer: @kbd{Ctrl-K I} indented it,
@kbd{Ctrl-O E} added a line, Search > Replace replaced.  Those commands
are now refused there and the status row says so, because the window
says it cannot be changed.

@item Ctrl-O E
The original put the help file's end-of-page mark on an empty row without
a line break, so the screen showed it on a line of its own while the file
written ran it into the start of the next line.  The mark now has a line of
its own in the file too, and one @kbd{Ctrl-U} takes that line back.
@end table

@section Short commands

@table @asis
@item Print, manual pages and compressed help files
@cindex print command, time limit
@cindex man, time limit
The original ran the print command, @command{man} and @command{gunzip}
through the shell and waited for them with no limit, the editor frozen
meanwhile.  Each now has a time limit (a minute to print, 30 seconds for
a page or an unpack), the window is drawn again while it runs, and one
that takes longer than a second is named on the status row.  On X11 and
Wayland, @key{Esc} ends it at once and the status row says
@samp{Esc: stopped waiting for} the command; nothing else follows, so no
``Not Installed'' box after Print and no ``No manual entry'' window after
Help, since you chose not to wait.  The keys typed before @key{Esc} are
still read by the editor afterwards.  A check the editor makes by itself
(the version of your Java before a Scala session, the desktop's font) does
not take your @key{Esc}: it stays for what you were doing.  In a terminal
the keyboard waits for the editor until the command ends.  A command that
hung used to leave no way out but killing the editor.
@end table

@section Commands a project folder names

@table @asis
@item The print command, compilers and debugger of a folder's xwperc or a project file
@cindex xwperc, commands from a project folder
@cindex project file, commands from
The original read an @file{xwperc} in the folder it was started in as
the whole configuration, and ran its print command, compiler lines and
debugger, and a project file's @code{CMP}, @code{CMPFLAGS},
@code{LDFLAGS} and @code{install:} lines, with no question (a Scala
project's @file{.bsp} build server is newer than the original, and is
treated the same way).  An @code{Exedir}, @code{FILES} or @code{LIBNAME}
value starting with @samp{-} or @samp{@@} was handed to the compiler as
an option or a response file; it is now passed as @file{./}@var{path}.
Now the first time such a command is about to run (a compile, a link, a
print, an install, a debugger start or a build server start) and
differs from your own setting, xwpe shows it and asks @samp{Run it},
@samp{Use mine} or @samp{Cancel}, and remembers the answer for that
folder and command in your state folder (@pxref{Folder commands}).  The
reason: the commands a folder's files name for a compile, a link, a
print, an install, a debugger start or a build-server start now ask
once, and a path a folder value hands to a program is never read as an
option or a response file.  Run and Make still run the folder's own
program, @code{Arguments} and @file{Makefile}, as they always did.

Not covered yet, and planned: where a folder's @code{EXENAME},
@code{Exedir}, @code{Executable} or @code{LIBNAME} puts the build's
output (it can be outside the folder, for example
@file{~/.local/bin}); a folder's @code{IncludePath} widening what the
assistant may read; and a debug session reading the folder's own
debugger start-up files (@file{.pdbrc}, @file{.perldb},
@file{jdb.ini}, @file{.gdbinit}-style files) and running its program,
as Run and Make do.
@end table

@section Colours on an X display

@table @asis
@item A full colormap
@cindex colormap, full
@cindex -pcmap
The original stopped at start with ``all colorcells allocated'' on a
palette (8-bit) display whose shared colormap other programs had filled,
unless it was started with @option{-pcmap}.  It now takes, for each
colour it cannot get, the nearest colour already in the colormap when one
is close enough to read as the same; when none is, it switches to a
private colormap by itself, as @option{-pcmap} does, and says so once on
stderr.  The reason: running with nearly the right colours is better than
not running.

@item A colour name the server does not know
The original stopped at start when an X resource named a colour the
server does not know.  It now reports the name on stderr and uses the
built-in colour for that entry, so one typing mistake costs one colour.
@end table
